            "TORUS, a 6 × 8 grid of quads split into triangles:\n    torus of revolution (tube 1, centre 2)\n      V =  48   E =  144   F =  96    χ = V−E+F =  0\n      Σ Kᵥ =  -7.993605777301127e-15      2πχ =  0.0\n      difference =  -7.993605777301127e-15\n      most positive defect =  0.770115751806955\n      most negative defect =  -0.7701157518069559\n      They cancel to machine precision.  4.06 found this torus has\n      K > 0 on the outside and K < 0 on the inside, and 5.06 showed\n      ∫K dA = 0 exactly.  Here the SAME cancellation happens with no\n      integral and no smoothness — just 48 vertices and their angles.\nTOPOLOGICAL, NOT GEOMETRIC.  Deform the icosahedron by scaling\nvertex i by (1 + i/20), destroying every symmetry it had:\n    deformed icosahedron\n      V =  12   E =  30   F =  20    χ = V−E+F =  2\n      Σ Kᵥ =  12.56637061435917      2πχ =  12.566370614359172\n      difference =  -1.7763568394002505e-15\n      individual defects, original  :\n                                      [1.0471975511965983,1.0471975511965983,\n                                       1.0471975511965983,1.0471975511965983]\n                                       ...\n      individual defects, deformed  :\n                                      [0.4420141363865504,0.48978355680221597,\n                                       0.5736968429832094,0.6192084365509976]\n                                       ...\n   Every vertex now has a different curvature, and the total is\n   unmoved.  Σ Kᵥ measures the topology and nothing else — which is\n   exactly the character 5.06 identified for a characteristic class.\nA CAVEAT ON 'EXACT'.  The identity Σ Kᵥ = 2πχ is exact as algebra;\nthe residuals above are of order 10⁻¹⁵, which is floating-point\nround-off in the arccosines, not discretisation error.  Refining\nthe mesh would not reduce a discretisation error here, because\nthere is none to reduce."
