            "COTANGENT LAPLACIAN on the icosahedron (12 × 12).\n   symmetric ?  max |L − Lᵀ| =  0.0\n   row sums (constants are in the kernel) : max |Σⱼ L_ij| =\n  2.220446049250313e-16\n   Both are structural, not numerical: the weight on edge ij is the\n   same seen from either end, and D was built as the row sum of W.\n   eigenvalues, sorted:\n       [0.0,1.596,1.596,1.596,3.464,3.464,3.464,3.464,3.464,4.178,4.178,4.178]\n   One zero (the constants), then a triple, a quintuple and a\n   triple — the dimensions of the icosahedral group's irreducible\n   representations.  On the smooth sphere the Laplacian's\n   eigenspaces are the spherical harmonics, of dimensions\n   2ℓ+1 = 1, 3, 5, 7, …; the mesh reproduces the first two and\n   then runs out of vertices.\nLINEAR PRECISION.  A planar fan: one interior vertex, six neighbours\nat irregular positions.\n   f =  3 x +  -2 y +  5    (Lf) at the interior vertex =\n       2.6645352591003757e-15\n   f =  1 x +  0 y +  0    (Lf) at the interior vertex =\n       -2.220446049250313e-16\n   f =  0 x +  1 y +  0    (Lf) at the interior vertex =\n       -5.551115123125783e-17\n   Zero to round-off for every linear f.  The cotangent weights are\n   not a plausible-looking guess: they are what the finite-element\n   derivation with piecewise-linear hat functions produces, and this\n   test is what they are for.  Uniform (graph) weights fail it on\n   any irregular mesh:\n      uniform-weight Laplacian on f = 3x − 2y + 5 :\n  0.10000000000000142    ≠ 0\n   The graph Laplacian sees only the combinatorics; the cotangent\n   Laplacian sees the geometry.  That is the difference between a\n   mesh as a graph and a mesh as a surface."
