    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": "## Summary\n\n$$K_v = 2\\pi - \\sum_i\\theta_i, \\qquad \\sum_v K_v = 2\\pi\\chi(M)$$\n\n**Gauss–Bonnet holds on four vertices.**\n\n| mesh | $V, E, F$ | $\\chi$ | $\\sum K_v$ | $2\\pi\\chi$ |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| tetrahedron | 4, 6, 4 | 2 | 12.5664 | 12.5664 |\n| octahedron | 6, 12, 8 | 2 | 12.5664 | 12.5664 |\n| icosahedron | 12, 30, 20 | 2 | 12.5664 | 12.5664 |\n| torus, $6\\times8$ grid | 48, 144, 96 | 0 | $-8\\times10^{-15}$ | 0 |\n\n3.08 needed a smooth surface and an integral. This needs neither, and the proof is a count: every triangle contributes $\\pi$ to the total angle sum, so $\\sum\\theta = \\pi F$, and $2\\pi V - \\pi F = 2\\pi(V - E + F)$ once $3F = 2E$ is used. The residuals above are round-off in the arccosines — **there is no discretisation error to refine away**.\n\n**The torus cancels, again.** Its most positive and most negative vertex defects are $\\pm0.7701$, summing to zero. 4.06 found this same torus has $K > 0$ outside and $K < 0$ inside; 5.06 integrated to get exactly zero. Here 48 vertices and their angles do it with no calculus at all.\n\n**Topological, not geometric.** Scaling icosahedron vertex $i$ by $(1 + i/20)$ destroys every symmetry — individual defects go from a uniform $1.0472$ to $0.442, 0.490, 0.574, 0.619, \\dots$ — and the total is unmoved at $2\\pi\\chi$. That is exactly the behaviour 5.06 identified for a characteristic class: a number no deformation of the geometry can change.\n\n**The cotangent Laplacian earns its weights.** On the icosahedron it is symmetric to $0.0$ and its row sums vanish to $2\\times10^{-16}$ — both structural, since the edge weight is the same from either end and $D$ is the row sum of $W$. Its spectrum is\n\n$$0,\\ \\underbrace{1.596\\ (\\times3)}_{\\ell=1},\\ \\underbrace{3.464\\ (\\times5)}_{\\ell=2},\\ 4.178\\ (\\times3),$$\n\nthe multiplicities $1, 3, 5$ of the first spherical harmonics, reproduced by twelve vertices before the mesh runs out of resolution.\n\n**Linear precision is the real test.** On a planar fan with six irregularly placed neighbours, the cotangent Laplacian annihilates $3x - 2y + 5$, $x$ and $y$ to round-off ($10^{-15}$ or better). The uniform graph Laplacian gives $0.1$ on the same function. **The graph Laplacian sees only the combinatorics; the cotangent Laplacian sees the geometry** — and the difference is the whole reason mesh processing uses the latter.\n\n**The honest limits.** Angle defect is a *total* quantity: it is a measure concentrated on vertices, not a pointwise curvature, and dividing by a vertex area to get a \"value\" is a choice with no canonical answer. Ricci curvature, parallel transport and geodesics all need extra structure to discretise. And there is a genuine no-free-lunch result: no discrete Laplacian on general triangle meshes is simultaneously symmetric, local, linearly precise and positive with a maximum principle. Something must be surrendered, and which thing depends on what the mesh is for.\n\n## Next\n\n**6.07 — Where Next** closes the journey: honest signposts to index theory, Ricci flow and the Poincaré conjecture, geometric analysis, and metric geometry in the sense of Gromov — the directions that start where Stage 6 stops.\n\n## References\n\n- Crane, K. (2018) *Discrete Differential Geometry: An Applied Introduction*. Carnegie Mellon University course notes. The best entry point by a distance: it builds discrete exterior calculus from scratch and is explicit about which smooth theorems survive and which do not.\n- Meyer, M., Desbrun, M., Schröder, P. and Barr, A.H. (2003) 'Discrete differential-geometry operators for triangulated 2-manifolds', in *Visualization and Mathematics III*. Berlin: Springer, pp. 35–57. Where the cotangent formula and the mixed-area normalisation were set out for graphics.\n- Pinkall, U. and Polthier, K. (1993) 'Computing discrete minimal surfaces and their conjugates', *Experimental Mathematics*, 2(1), pp. 15–36. The cotangent Laplacian's origin as a variational object, applied to 3.09's minimal surfaces.\n- Wardetzky, M., Mathur, S., Kälberer, F. and Grinspun, E. (2007) 'Discrete Laplace operators: no free lunch', in *Proceedings of the Eurographics Symposium on Geometry Processing*, pp. 33–37. The no-go theorem quoted above, stated and proved.\n- Bobenko, A.I. and Suris, Y.B. (2008) *Discrete Differential Geometry: Integrable Structure*. Graduate Studies in Mathematics 98. Providence: American Mathematical Society. The other tradition — discretise so that integrable structure is preserved, rather than so that convergence is fast.\n\n---\n*No ★ here: none of this notebook's references were consulted directly, so all are given from knowledge. Every code cell was executed. The mesh toolkit — angles, defects, edge counting, cotangent weights — is written from scratch in the cells above; Maxima has no mesh support. Two things are stated but not proved here: the no-free-lunch theorem, and the convergence of angle defect to $\\int K\\,dA$ under refinement.*",
      "metadata": {}
    }
  ]
}